  "content": {
    "id": "p7-q7-root",
    "type": "question",
    "label": "7",
    "prompt": "Explain why $(3-x)^2$ and $(x-3)^2$ are the same.",
    "layout": "list",
    "columns": null,
    "questionDiagrams": [],
    "children": [],
    "answer": {
      "short": "The bracketed expressions are negatives of each other, and squaring removes the negative sign: $(3-x)^2=[-(x-3)]^2=(x-3)^2$.",
      "worked": "$$\\begin{align*}(3-x)^2&=3^2+2(3)(-x)+(-x)^2\\\\(3-x)^2&=9-6x+x^2\\\\(x-3)^2&=x^2+2(x)(-3)+(-3)^2\\\\(x-3)^2&=x^2-6x+9\\end{align*}$$\nThese expansions have the same terms. Also, $3-x=-(x-3)$, so squaring gives $[ -(x-3)]^2=(x-3)^2$.",
      "solutionDiagrams": []
    },
    "answerSpaceMm": 24
  },
